1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,720
Agent work usually starts with a vague prompt and ends with an answer nobody can verify.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:10,760
The Wringer changes that.

3
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:16,580
This is the live production site, and ChatGPT's in-app browser has discovered six native WebMCP tools.

4
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:21,500
First, the agent creates a case file.

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,880
The same form a human uses updates instantly.

6
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:31,360
Next, it reviews the case.

7
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:38,980
The Wringer catches two real gaps: there is no machine check, and no falsifiable expected signal.

8
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:44,350
The agent repairs both in the same shared state.

9
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:49,280
Now it stages a one-dollar Quick Attack.

10
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:55,100
Watch the boundary. Confirmation required. Checkout not started. Charged: false.

11
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:01,820
The agent can understand the product, improve the work, and prepare execution, but it cannot spend for me.

12
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:07,200
The normal interface still works without WebMCP, and both humans and agents use the same application services.

13
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:10,960
This is not remote-controlled clicking. The website publishes precise capabilities, the agent invokes them directly, and The Wringer keeps the human in control.
